  "id": "COMPOUND:688 - DISEASE:1010",
  "verbalization": "Methazolamide is implicated in the pathogenesis of Enterocolitis, Pseudomembranous, through a complex network of molecular interactions involving immunoglobulin kappa variable pseudogenes. The evidence suggests that Methazolamide affects these pseudogenes, which in turn contribute to the development of the condition. Specifically, the pseudogenes appear to act as mediators, linking Methazolamide to a cascade of events that culminate in the manifestation of Enterocolitis, Pseudomembranous. This mechanism is supported by multiple paths, all of which highlight a shared pathway involving immunoglobulin kappa variable pseudogenes and their downstream effects on the condition. The presence of multiple pseudogenes in this context suggests a potential redundancy or compensatory mechanism, which may explain the observed variability in disease expression. The integration of these findings underscores the role of these pseudogenes as key players in the pathophysiology of the condition, with Methazolamide acting as a catalyst in their activation or dysfunction.",
